  "summary": "An Aboriginal Australian art style characterized by intricate patterns of dots forming Dreaming stories and cultural knowledge.",
  "description": "Created using acrylic paint on canvas, it features dense dotwork patterns representing ancestral journeys and sacred sites. Developed as a way to share cultural stories while maintaining secrecy of sacred knowledge.",
  "culturalContext": "In the late 1960s, the Australian Government moved several different groups living in the Western Desert region to Papunya, 240 km (150 mi) north-west of Alice Springs in the Northern Territory, to remove them from cattle lands and assimilate them into western culture. These displaced groups were primarily Pintupi, Luritja, Walpiri, Arrernte, and Anmatyerre groups.",
